{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 240,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Kurt Godel broke everything. Most famously, he \nbroke mathematics with his incompleteness theorem,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7200,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "showing that all formal systems contain true \nstatements that can’t be proved within said  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11520,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "system. In studying for his US naturalization \ninterview, he claims to have discovered a legal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 18000,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "means by which the US republic could be \ntransitioned into a fascist dictatorship.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24560,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "His good friend Albert Einstein along with Oskar \nMorgenstern insisted on accompanying him to his  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31280,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interview in the hope of stopping him blurting out \nhis proof - which he very nearly did anyway. Oh,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38880,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and speaking of Einstein—once, as a birthday \npresent, Godel gave him a time machine  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45680,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe—a solution to the Einstein equations that \nproved that even general relativity was broken."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58800,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We've got a couple of quick announcements \nbefore we start. First, our eternal battle  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63840,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "against the algorithm continues. The best \nway to encourage YouTube to share our videos  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69040,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is to like and comment. Doing both really \nmakes a difference. And if you're new here,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75120,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "subscribe, hit the bell, and introduce \nyourself in the comments. We're friendly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80320,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Next up, we’re excited to launch our Universe in \na Black Hole T-shit and Hoodie. If you believe  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84400,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’re trapped inside a black hole where space \nand time swapped roles and the Big Bang was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88320,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just the other side of a collapse then this is \nthe shirt for you. We’re also still offer up our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92560,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Black hole light curves desktop and gaming \nmat that shows the ways light is warped by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96640,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the gravity of a black hole. Plus, our UV-glow \nKerr rotating black hole hoodies, shirt and dark  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101680,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy mugs and shirts are all still available. \nLink’s in the description. Now, onto the episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106800,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s not an exaggeration to say that Godel and \nEinstein were best friends. The aging Einstein  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112400,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "once said that the only reason he continued \nto show up at Princeton was for his walks with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117600,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Godel. And so when the occasion of Einstein’s \n70th birthday approached, it’s no surprise that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124240,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Godel enthusiastically agreed to contribute \nto a book of tributes to Einstein’s work.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130880,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At first Godel thought to write a \nreflection on the relationship between  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 134720,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Einsteinian spacetime and the ideas of the \nphilosopher Immanuel Kant. As was his wont,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140560,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Godel fell down a rabbit hole and in the process \nof writing this one essay, he came to understand  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146320,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "general relativity as well as anyone in the \nworld. And then he stumbled on something deeply  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152480,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "worrying—an inconsistency at the core of his \nbest friend’s theory. The rabbit hole deepened."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159200,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The deadline for the essay came and went—but \nso important was Godel’s contribution that the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163840,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "book had to wait. When it was finally done, the \nreason for Godel’s obsession became clear. He’d  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170000,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "found a solution to the field equation—the \ncore of general relativity—that proves that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175600,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Einstein’s theory by itself does not guarantee \na clean chain of cause and effect that it was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183280,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "supposed to. He’d built a spacetime metric—a \nuniverse—where any point in space and time  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190640,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there are return loops that bring you \nback to the location before you left."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197120,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By the time of Godel’s discovery we already \nknew of solutions to the Einstein equation  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202240,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that allow time travel. But all of them \nrequire something that can be argued is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208640,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "impossible—negative energy density. So, just \nadd a prohibition of this impossible stuff  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215920,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the broken solutions go away. This \nprohibition is called the weak energy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220960,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "condition. With the Einstein equation plus \nthe weak energy condition, a deterministic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226480,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe with unambiguous causal ordering \nseemed guaranteed. That is until Godel. He  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234240,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "found a new way to time travel and to break causal \nstructure—no impossible ingredients required—and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242080,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the result is the Godel universe, where time \ntravel isn’t just possible—it’s inevitable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 249600,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s take a trip into the Godel universe, \nfirst by time traveling back to relativity 101."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256960,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In relativity theory, space and time \nare not cleanly separated—they’re  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261200,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "both part of a 4-dimensional object called \nspacetime. Depending on motion and gravity,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 268640,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space and time become mixed, trading into each \nother. The classic way to depict this is with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 274400,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a spacetime diagram, with just one dimension \nof space. Everything moves up through time,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 279840,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but if you’re also moving through space you \nmove on a sloped line. Shallower is faster,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284800,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and 45 degrees represents the speed of light. \nBecause nothing can travel faster than light,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 289680,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you can only ever influence parts of the future \nin this cone—your forward lightcone. Meanwhile,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 295920,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your past lightcone represents the parts of \nthe past that could have had an influence on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 300720,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you. No possible signal could connect \nyou to regions outside these cones."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307440,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So you can travel or can have traveled \nany path inside these cones. We call  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 313920,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these “time-like paths” because on these, \nyour motion up through time is greater  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318880,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than your motion sideways through space. \nPaths into the forbidden zones are called  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 323680,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space-like—more space than time is traversed \nin these, and that, supposedly, is impossible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329760,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But let’s try anyway. Say we jump in our \nrocketship and accelerate away from the Earth,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 334720,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quickly reaching a large fraction of \nlightspeed. Relativity tells us that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 339280,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "my time axis shifts relative to the direction of \nthe axis back on Earth. From Earth’s perspective,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 346560,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "my time and space coordinates get mixed—my \nclock slows and my length contracts. If  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 352560,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I actually reach the speed of light, my clock \nstops, and if I can go faster … well, from the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358560,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perspective of some observers my clock actually \nreverses and I appear to move backwards in time.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 364720,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is equivalent to breaking free of \nyour light cone. Once you can do that,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370400,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the past is accessible to you. And a long time ago \nwe did some episodes that showed how this works."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377760,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The impossibility of superluminal motion is \ndirectly connected to the impossibility of time  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 383120,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "travel in special relativity—both are baked \nin via the Lorentz transformation. This is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 389280,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "absolute protection of the causal structure \nof spacetime in the absence of gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 394640,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gravity changes things because it bends the path \nof light, so it can tip our future light cone. For  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402160,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "example, approaching the black hole light paths \nare bent, and so our lightcone bends towards  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 410080,
    "dDurationMs": 8560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the event horizon until our entire accessible \nfuture lies inside the black hole. Below the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418640,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "event horizon the light cone tips fully sideways, \nwhich means the “down” direction becomes your new  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425520,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time coordinate. Now, if you could turn around and \nkeep your lightcone aligned like this you really  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 433280,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could travel back in time. But in a black hole \nyou can’t turn around your future is only down."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439840,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, let me actually revise that. In a rotating \nblack hole—a Kerr black hole—there’s a region  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446080,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "deep within where angular motion becomes \ntimelike. Space is spinning so quickly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 452240,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that a circular path gets you back to \nwhere you started at the same time as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 457440,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you left. We call this a closed timelike \ncurve—a CTC. It’s timelike in this case  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 465520,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because you never violated relativity \nby leaving your forward light cone.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 471120,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These regions of the Kerr black hole may not \nreally even exist, and in any case it’s a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 477280,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pretty useless sort of time travel, because \nyour stuck behind the event horizon anyway."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482320,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can force closed timelike curves into \nthe accessible parts of the universe by bending  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 488480,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spacetime in various exotic ways(wormholes, warp \ndrives etc) but in all cases these require some  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 494880,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sort of negative energy. But then came Godel \nof course. Even before Roy Kerr came up with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 501680,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the rotating black hole solution, Godel realized \nthat rotational motion was the key to time travel."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509760,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The general effect he relies on is called \nframe dragging—the twisting of spacetime  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515440,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "due to a rotating mass. And we’ve measured \nthis. Gravity probe B sent a gyroscope in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522480,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "orbit around the Earth. A gyroscope is supposed \nto always point in the same direction. In fact,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 528000,
    "dDurationMs": 8720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Godel calls the device an “inertial compass” in \nhis paper. But the gyroscope in Gravity Probe B  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 536720,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "slowly swiveled from its original pointing \nas it orbited due to both the gravitational  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 542560,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "curvature and the twisted spacetime—the \nframe dragging caused by Earth’s rotation.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549840,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This angular shift is accompanied by a \ntime shift, but in the case of Earth’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 555760,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "frame dragging is a far too weak an effect for \nthat component to be measured. But in principle,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 562320,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if you travel around Earth the right way \nyour clock slows due to the angular motion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568080,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s never enough to freeze time altogether \nthough. Even around a rapidly rotating black  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 573680,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole there are no closed timelike curves on \nthe outside of a black hole—only deep within."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 580240,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But Godel realised that this frame dragging \neffect could still break causality if it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586320,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "happens in a spacetime where the events \ncan add up. Now the key is to describe a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 592400,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spacetime which has a fundamental twist to it \neverywhere—all points feel frame dragging—not  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 600000,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just around a single spinning objects. We say \nit has global vorticity. All spacetime points  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 608160,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rotate relative to neighbors—not like every \nspot spinning, but rather like it's a vortex  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 614400,
    "dDurationMs": 8640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of worldlines in an infinite 4-D spacetime that \nhas no center. There are other requirements—a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623040,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "negative curvature “hyperbolic” geometry is \nneeded to allow the center-less rotation, and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 630480,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a balance of smooth positive matter and negative \ndark energy to keep the universe static in size."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 637840,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But tuned right, Godel showed that such a \nuniverse can be full of closed timelike curves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 645200,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s see what it looks like. This saddle shape \nis a representation of a 2-D hyperbolic space.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 651680,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’re reserving the real Z direction for time so \nthat we can stack these planes to represent a 4-D  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658480,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe. Zooming in it looks flat locally, but \nthe negative curvature is still there. You don’t  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 665760,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "notice anything weird moving through this space, \neven though the underlying twisting is there.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672400,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You notice this when time moves forward, and \nneighboring particles follow twisted paths."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 679680,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "From any point in this space, as you travel \noutwards your lightcone tilts relative to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 686320,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the reference frame of your starting point. And \nbeyond a certain distance—the Godel horizon—it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 692240,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has tilted far enough to allow travel into \nthe past. Or at least, to claw back some  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 699440,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the time you spent traveling. But with a \ncarefully planned route, traveling in a loop,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705600,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you can steer your future light cone to \neventually contain your starting point."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 711200,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another way to think about it is that, by \nmixing time with the angular coordinate,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 716640,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your motion in a loop takes the place of and \neven reverses temporal motion. That’s right,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 724400,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Godel invented the time-turner—turn enough \nand you travel backwards in time. But with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 731200,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "restrictions. You have to travel out beyond \nthe Godel horizon from your starting point,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 735840,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which means you can’t just spin on \nthe spot and move into the past."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 739440,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The time-traveling possibilities of the Godel \nuniverse are fun, but they aren’t the real point.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 746080,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The real point is that this solution to the \nEinstein equation is a counter-example to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 751920,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "idea that baseline general relativity—the Einstein \nequation plus the weak energy condition—are enough  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 757280,
    "dDurationMs": 8720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to ensure sensible universes. Before this, any \nvalid GR spacetime could be sliced into layers of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 766000,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "consecutive “nows”, where the exact configuration \nof everything—the particles, the fields—of each of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772640,
    "dDurationMs": 9680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "those slices can be used to generates the next \nslice. This perfect  global determinism isn’t  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 782320,
    "dDurationMs": 8960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "true in Godel’s universe, and the past and future \nbecome tangled and not clearly definable. Godel  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 791280,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "proved that general relativity doesn’t guarantee \nspacetimes that have a rigid causal ordering. At  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 797440,
    "dDurationMs": 8880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "least in some cases, like in Godel’s universe, \nwe can’t say whether A caused B or B caused A."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 806320,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What Godel really showed was that the prohibition \nagainst negative energy wasn’t enough to ensure  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 812640,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sensible universes. Others came along and \nproposed new conditions. “Global Hyperbolicity”  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 820640,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was proposed as an explicit requirement—which \nhas nothing to do with hyperbolic geometries.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 827360,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It basically states that for any physically \nreasonable solution to general relativity,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 832880,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "any constant-time slice must fully determine \nthe next constant time slice, and that has to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 840880,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be true however you do that time slicing. Then \ncame Stephen Hawking’s Chronology Protection  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 847200,
    "dDurationMs": 8960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Conjecture, which argues that any spacetime that \nallows closed timelike curves is unstable—feedback  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 856160,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from time-traveling vacuum energy will cause \nthe ultimate reverb and collapse the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 864160,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, that’s really what Godel gave his \nfriend Einstein for his birthday—the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 867840,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "impetus for future generations of physicists \nto keep working on Einstein’s greatest theory.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 873760,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Godel didn’t break GR, but he showed \nthe rest of us where some of the few  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 878960,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "remaining cracks are. Cracks which may, in \ntime, lead new Einsteins and new Godels to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 884240,
    "dDurationMs": 18240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "deeper theories. Really quite the birthday \ngift, Godel’s time-turning spacetime."
    } ]
  } ]
}
